CTYPE(3)                       P32W Unix                        CTYPE(3)

NAME
       ctype, isalpha, isupper, islower, isdigit, isalnum - classify

SYNOPSIS
       #include <ctype.h>

       int isalpha(int c);
       int isupper(int c);
       int islower(int c);
       int isdigit(int c);
       int isalnum(int c);

DESCRIPTION
       ctype.h declares functions that classify a character c by
       the ASCII table: each returns non-zero (1) if c is of its
       class, and 0 if not. Values outside 0 to 127, EOF (-1)
       among them, belong to no class.

       isalpha    a letter: A-Z, a-z
       isupper    an upper-case letter: A-Z
       islower    a lower-case letter: a-z
       isdigit    a decimal digit: 0-9
       isalnum    a letter or a digit

       The other classes are on isspace(3): isspace, isxdigit,
       ispunct, isprint, isgraph and iscntrl; toupper(3) converts
       the case of letters.

EXAMPLES
       while (isspace(*s)) s++;
       if (isalpha(*s) || *s == '_')
           ...                     /* the start of a name */

SEE ALSO
       isspace(3), toupper(3), atoi(3)

BUGS
       They are functions, not macros or table look-ups, so each
       test costs a call. There are no locales: bytes 128 to 255 are
       never letters. char is signed, so such a byte passed as a
       char arrives negative, which is harmless here but not on
       other systems: pass (unsigned char)c. There is no isascii,
       isblank or toascii.
